body:
@aothms , 

have you seen the progress on OCC dev wrt [fuzzy boolean operations](http://dev.opencascade.org/index.php?q=node/1056)?

Could be very relevant...

`undeclared identifier` means in this case there is no variable in your scope with this name, see how this is done for `name` and `guid` https://github.com/IfcOpenShell/IfcOpenShell/blob/master/src/ifcgeom/IfcGeomFunctions.cpp#L880

Since LongName is an attribute of SpatialStructureElement, you'll need to cast to a subtype first. Something like the following (untested).

```
std::string longname;
if (product->is(IfcSchema::Type::IfcSpatialStructureElement)) {
    longname = static_cast<IfcSchema::IfcSpatialStructureElement*>(product)->LongName()
}
```

Let's forget about the 2nd approach, since you're here already.

A file mmapped in two processes is shared between the process (less memory is used). See for example the following code in java ....

import java.io.File;
import java.io.RandomAccessFile;
import java.nio.MappedByteBuffer;
import java.nio.channels.FileChannel;

//lsof : IfcConvert 23122  user  mem    REG    8,5 52923163  6034972 BOREL_IFC.ifc

public class Test {
    /*
     \* This Java application should be called with two args :
     \* args[0] is the pathname of IfcConvert
     \* args[1] is the pathname of the file to convert 
     \* 
     \* If the ifc file is big enough you'll see with lsof (lsof -f -p pid)
     \* that the file is mapped in memory in both  java and
     \* IfcConvert. 
     \* Moreover this memory segment is shared between the two processes as 
     \* their address are the same in each process.
     \* Memory is thus allocated only one time in the mmap scenario instead of two in the file/open 
        \* one.
     */

```
 public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
     RandomAccessFile file =  new RandomAccessFile(args[1],"r");
     MappedByteBuffer mmap = file.getChannel().map(FileChannel.MapMode.READ_ONLY,0,file.length());


     Runtime runtime = Runtime.getRuntime();
     Process p = runtime. exec(args);
     p.waitFor();
     System.out.println(p.exitValue());


 }
```

}

Thanks for posting the code so that others can benefit. Keep in mind though that not necessarily all products passed to this function will be spaces. The default when converting to SVG is to only include spaces, but if you would invoke the executable like this `IfcConvert in.ifc out.svg --include --entities IfcWall IfcWindow` then walls and windows would be processed by this function, which are not IfcSpatialStructureElement and the code will crash.

It is better to make this cast conditional, something like this:

```
std::string longname = "";
if (product->is(IfcSchema::Type::IfcSpatialStructureElement)) {
    longname = static_cast<IfcSchema::IfcSpatialStructureElement*> (product)->LongName();
}
```

@jf--- was right. The new fuzzy boolean ops seem to do the job. Note that you need a recent version of OCC for which unfortunately no Open Cascade Community Edition version has been released yet. 

Fix for the styled item is in as well.

You can check the code here https://github.com/aothms/IfcOpenShell. Will merge to upstream later.

@aothms , excited to hear so... thanks for sharing that finding...

Are you tracking performance for IfcOpenShell? 
Bool ops were known to be pretty slow, I was thrilled to see the fuzzy bool ops to be both more robust and more than an order of magnitude quicker...

I'm able to generate proper non-crashing DAE by adding openAsset() and closeAsset() calls to `ColladaSerializer::ColladaExporter::startDocument`:

``` c++
    COLLADASW::Asset asset(&stream);
    asset.openAsset();
    // ...
    asset.closeAsset();
}
```

Would this be a good fix for this instead of starting hunting changes in OpenCOLLADA and the old code has been working accidentally?

- So you preference would be `UpperCamelCase` for classes and `lowercase_with_underscores` for functions and variables? And `UpperCamelCase.h` (following the name of the class) for files for majority of the time?
- BTW how about American vs British English? I think I've seen British English preferred so this is probably good to document.
- I also noticed that throughout the project #includes are in reverse order in what I'm used to in majority of projects. e.g.
  SomeClass.cpp:

``` c++
#include <standardheader>
#include <ThirdPartyFile>
#include "IfcSomeFile.h"
#include "SomeClass.H"

SomeClass::SomeClass()
{
    //...
```

Is this intentional?
- BTW personally I think MSVC's (supported by all notable compilers nowadays) `#pragma once` inclusion guard is great, but I totally understood if we want to stay with the classic guards.
- Also one minor thing I was wondering was that instead of

``` c++
#include <COLLADASWStreamWriter.h>
#include <Standard_Version.hxx>
```

would we like to have

``` c++
#include <opencollada/COLLADASWStreamWriter.h>
#include <oce/Standard_Version.hxx>
```

I think that would be a good place. Question also is what products to include. If only including products that will be emitted, you have the risk of users processing types separately being unable to overlay their models geometrically. So, I'm guessing something like this would work:

```
            double minx = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
            ...
            IfcSchema::IfcProduct::list::ptr products = ifc_file->entitiesByType<IfcSchema::IfcProduct>();
            for (IfcSchema::IfcProduct::list::it it = products->begin(); it != products->end(); ++it) {
                IfcSchema::IfcProduct* product = *it;
                if (product->hasObjectPlacement) {
                    // This is a bit buggy, but it's important that the trsf is newly initialized every time, or they will be concatenated (and wrongly stored in the cache!).
                    gp_Trsf trsf;
                    if (kernel.convert(product->ObjectPlacement(), trsf)) {
                        const gp_XYZ& position = trsf.TranslationPart();
                        minx = (std::min)(minx, position.X());
                        ...
                    }
                }
            }
```

Some models contain explicit BoundingBox representations for products. For most other representations it can potentially be straightforward to compute them, without too much computation, except for things like boolean operands where computing the boundingbox is basically equivalent to the actual geometry processing.

If the goal is mostly to workaround issues like https://github.com/IfcOpenShell/IfcOpenShell/issues/8 and maximizing floating point precision it shouldn't really matter whether to use the bounding box or placement origin. Most of these cases come from a very large translation at the IfcSite or IfcBuilding level. For example in the case of https://github.com/IfcOpenShell/IfcOpenShell/issues/8 . If this offset would be introduced within the geometries the model would be seriously flawed.

```
#1421= IFCCARTESIANPOINT((114502766.318516,1185944009.,0.));
#1423= IFCDIRECTION((0.995788774458495,0.0916772417912352,0.));
#1425= IFCAXIS2PLACEMENT3D(#1421,#19,#1423);
#1426= IFCLOCALPLACEMENT($,#1425);
#1427= IFCSITE('3o91zj$Gr6cQ_i1EP5O_03',#41,'0214 41 1 00',$,'',#1426,$,'14323',.ELEMENT.,(59,39,52,987060),(10,47,40,726547),62050.,'0214 41 1 00',$);
```

From that point of view an alternative way of alleviating this problem would be an option to ignore Site and Building-level placements. That is also quickly to implement and might even work more predictably to users.

@aothms @Stinkfist0 Thanks for the quick reply!
The commit fixes that issue, and now it fails with some different boost errors:

<img width="1483" alt="screen shot 2016-03-24 at 9 15 07 am" src="https://cloud.githubusercontent.com/assets/96500/14005733/102a36d8-f1a1-11e5-9ce7-f61a017cd9ce.png">

@Stinkfist0 Using `unordered_map` turns out to yield a major improvement gain in terms of memory usage and processing time. Thanks for this! I will see if adapting the maps in the `IfcFile` class [1] offers improvements as well. I already thought having the map-by-id in a vector if ids are contiguous to have constant lookup times, but I guess a hash-table works for both contiguous and non-contiguous instance names. Although ordering might still make sense there when enumerating instances?

[1] https://github.com/IfcOpenShell/IfcOpenShell/blob/master/src/ifcparse/IfcFile.h#L35

